    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000154208439": {
      "title": "Research Security Manager",
      "description": "The Office for Research delivers professional research support services with functions spanning grants, development, ethics and integrity, policy, performance and reporting. Griffith University is one of Australia’s largest universities. It is research intensive, inclusive, innovative, progressive, deeply engaged in the Asia-Pacific region, and committed to ensuring it contributes to society through research that provides social dividends. We are seeking an experienced and strategic Research Security Manager to play a pivotal role in safeguarding the University's research enterprise while enabling world-class research collaboration and innovation. As a trusted expert and adviser within the Office for Research, you will lead the University's approach to research security, advising academic and professional staff on how to navigate an increasingly complex regulatory and geopolitical landscape. You will provide expert guidance on secure international engagement, defence export controls, sanctions compliance, foreign interference risks and other government security requirements that impact research activities. This is a unique opportunity to influence institutional policy, build organisational capability and support research excellence through a proactive and collaborative approach to security governance. Key responsibilities include but not limited to: Serve as the University's primary contact for research security matters, liaising with regulatory agencies and overseeing compliance reporting obligations. Fulfil the role of Institutional Security Officer , supporting the University's obligations under the Defence Science Partnership and Defence Industry Security Program frameworks. Provide expert advice on legislation and regulatory requirements relating to defence export controls, autonomous sanctions, foreign influence and foreign interference. Manage licences, permits, technology control plans and compliance records, ensuring accurate reporting and documentation. Develop and maintain policies, procedures and governance frameworks that promote best-practice research security compliance. Conduct due diligence assessments and research security risk analyses to support informed decision-making and risk mitigation. Design and deliver guidance, training, resources and digital tools that enhance research security awareness and capability across the University. Build effective relationships and communicate complex compliance requirements clearly to researchers, HDR candidates, professional staff and external stakeholders. About you \nThe successful candidate will have: An ability to obtain and maintain a Negative Vetting 1 (NV1) Security Clearance (or higher) in accordance with the University's Defence Industry Security Program (DISP) obligations. A bachelor's or postgraduate qualification in a relevant discipline, together with at least five years' relevant professional experience, preferably in Engineering, Computer Science, Physical Sciences, Biomedical Sciences, International Trade, Law, or a related field; or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. You will also have: Demonstrated knowledge of legislative and regulatory frameworks relating to research security, defence export controls, autonomous sanctions, foreign interference and foreign influence. Experience developing, implementing and monitoring compliance, governance or risk management frameworks within a complex organisation. Proven ability to conduct due diligence and risk assessments, analyse complex information, and provide practical, risk-based advice. Strong policy development and procedural documentation skills, with the ability to interpret and apply legislation in an operational environment. Excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills, including the ability to deliver training, provide advice and build productive relationships with internal and external stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to manage sensitive information with discretion, sound judgement and a high degree of professionalism. Desirable Experience working within the higher education, research, government, defence, national security or regulatory sectors. Knowledge of the Defence Industry Security Program (DISP), Defence Science Partnership framework, export controls, sanctions compliance or related research security programs. Experience engaging with Commonwealth Government agencies and regulatory authorities. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full-time base salary for a HEW Level 9 is in the range $137,165 - $142,070 per annum, plus 17% employer superannuation. The total FTE package is in the range $160,483 - $166,222 per annum. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About you section above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights.  Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Successful candidates may be subject to a criminal history check. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Dr Claudia Arango, Deputy Director – Research Excellence, Performance and Systems at c.arango@griffith.edu.au . For further application and recruitment advice, please contact Theresa Burke, Talent Acquisition Partner on t.burke@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Friday, 23 October 2026 at 11.59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000153876841": {
      "title": "HR Business Partner",
      "description": "About the opportunity  The HR Business Partner is a trusted advisor who collaborates with Griffith University’s leadership to deliver effective and efficient HR services. Supporting the Senior HR Business Partner within the Health Group, Griffith University’s largest academic group, this role partners with leaders across a diverse portfolio of disciplines, research institutes, clinics, and professional services. The Health Group is committed to improving health outcomes through excellence in education, research, and community impact. Leveraging data and HR best practices, the HR Business Partner implements people strategies that enhance workforce capability, engagement, and compliance while supporting the Group’s strategic objectives and the University’s broader goals. Your key responsibilities include: Partner with the Senior HR Business Partner to execute on key workforce operational activities Provide timely and practical advice on employee related issues (e.g performance management, case management, conflict resolution, coaching) Support workforce planning activities & contribute to change management initiatives  Promote a positive and inclusive workplace culture  Maintain accurate and compliant HR Records Contribute to HR projects and initiatives & collaborate with internal stakeholders  Influencing leaders across the University to foster a culture of belonging, integrity and commitment What you will bring to this role Knowledge of contemporary human resource practices and related legislation and industrial instruments Demonstrated ability to provide advice and manage the full range of HR issues High level communication, interpersonal and negotiation skills Exceptional computer literacy with an ability to use complex HRIS and related reporting/analytics tools A degree in Human Resource Management or a related field, and relevant knowledge and experience in human resource management and industrial relations in a diverse and complex organisation. What we can offer This is a continuing opportunity and can be offered as full time or part time and will be based at the Gold Coast campus. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations.  Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 8 Salary – Base range - $117,579.27 - $132,273.33 + 17% super. Full package range - $137,567.74 - $154,759.79. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 18th October at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why.  \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them.  Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Human Resources"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000153627389": {
      "title": "The Susan McClary Research Fellowship in Musicology",
      "description": "Fellowship Description \n Queensland Conservatorium Griffith University (QCGU) is pleased to announce a call for applications for the inaugural Susan McClary Research Fellowship in Musicology. The Fellowship, generously funded by Professor Sally Macarthur, is named in honour of esteemed musicologist Susan McClary. Professor McClary’s work has profoundly shaped the way music is understood within contemporary cultural studies. She has demonstrated that music is not only an aesthetic art form but also a powerful social and cultural practice, carrying meaning about identity, history, and society. The Fellowship aims to build on the spirt of McClary’s groundbreaking work. For the purposes of this Fellowship, musicology is defined as the academic and scholarly study of music, encompassing its history, theory, cultural contexts and social meaning. It asks not only how music is made and understood, but how it reflects, shapes and is shaped by the people, places and communities around it. Please see below for a longer statement on the discipline of musicology. Through this Fellowship, the recipient will be supported to undertake meaningful research that contributes to the field of musicology, enriches the intellectual and creative life of the Conservatorium, and strengthens national and international scholarship in this important field. The Susan McClary Research Fellowship in Musicology will support one recipient for a period of up to one academic year, creating a dedicated opportunity for focused scholarly research within Queensland Conservatorium Griffith University. The annual value of the stipend is $40,000 plus $10,000 for research-related expenses. QCGU is one of Australia's leading centres for music education, performance and research. With a vibrant community of researchers, educators and creative practitioners, QCGU is committed to advancing excellence in artistic and scholarly endeavour. The successful recipient will be known as the Susan McClary Fellow in Musicology for the duration of the award and will be expected to: Undertake a clearly defined research project in musicology Contribute to the scholarly life of QCGU through research engagement activities Produce research outcomes consistent with the objectives of the Fellowship Participate in reasonable reporting requirements associated with the award Acknowledge the Susan McClary Research Fellowship in resulting publications and outputs This Fellowship is awarded for a period of up to one academic year . Eligibility and selection Applicants must: Hold a doctoral qualification relevant to musicology (please refer to the Statement on Musicology below) Demonstrate a strong research track record appropriate to career stage Submit a high-quality research proposal aligned with the objectives of the Fellowship Demonstrate the potential to make a significant contribution to musicological scholarship Show the capacity to successfully complete the proposed project within the Fellowship period Applications will be assessed on: Academic qualifications and achievements Research track record and scholarly impact Quality, originality and feasibility of the proposed research Alignment with the purpose of the Susan McClary Research Fellowship Likely contribution to musicology and to the research culture of QCGU Please note that recipients of the Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship in Musicology are not eligible for the Susan McClary Fellowship, and vice versa. Fellowship Funding The Fellowship includes: An annual stipend of $40,000 Up to $10,000 for approved research-related expenses The Fellowship is a competitive research award and is not an offer of employment with Griffith University. The stipend does not constitute salary, and the recipient will not be appointed to a salaried academic position. The recipient will be provided with access to an email address and the Conservatorium’s library facilities. The recipient will also have access to shared desk space in the Conservatorium’s Research Hub. Statement on the discipline of Musicology Musicology is the critical study of music as a human and more-than-human phenomenon. It is a constantly changing, evolving discipline, but tends to be distinguished from research by composer and performer practitioners through the methods it uses to conduct its work. Whereas composers and performers produce knowledge through performing and composing music, musicologists generate knowledge through methods drawn not only from music practice—such as the ethnomusicologist working as a practitioner in the music of another culture—but also from the humanities, social sciences, and increasingly the sciences, with the aim of producing knowledge about music rather than knowledge through its performance or composition. Musicology is an interdisciplinary inquiry into music as a site where sound, body, technology, power, identity, and affect intersect and, more recently, where agency is understood as distributed across human and non-human phenomena. It draws on close analysis of sonic and notational texts, pays ethnographic attention to musical communities, undertakes archival and historiographical work, and utilises theoretical frameworks from critical theory and philosophy—including non-representational paradigms such as new materialism and agential realism—as well as disability studies, postcolonial thought, and media studies. The field has moved decisively away from positivist or formalist orientations towards an understanding of music not as a self-contained aesthetic object but as a relational practice, always embedded in bodies, technologies, institutions, and histories of inequality. Through recent developments it has moved beyond simply describing music's structures or charting its histories to interrogating the very conditions under which musical knowledge is produced, drawing richly from the most destabilising currents in contemporary thought. With this orientation as its foundation—and springing from the pioneering work of Susan McClary, who understands music as itself a form of cultural interpretation—Queensland Conservatorium Griffith University seeks to attract musicologists for this Fellowship whose work embodies this spirit of critical and disciplinary transformation. Applicants should identify, first and foremost, as musicologists or ethnomusicologists, and their work should demonstrate familiarity with the disciplinary transformations outlined above, while showing clearly how their proposed project will advance the field through innovative, interdisciplinary methods. \n\n How to Apply Please do not select the “APPLY” button.  Applications must be submitted to QCGU@griffith.edu.au including the following documentation: Cover letter outlining your interest in the Fellowship and suitability against the eligibility and selection criteria (max 1000 words) Research proposal outlining the proposed project and anticipated outcomes and significance (max 1000 words) Current curriculum vitae, including a list of publications and relevant scholarly outputs Contact details for at least two academic referees Applications close :  Thursday, 29 October 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. At Griffith, we are committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and encourages applications from people of all backgrounds. Enquiries For further information regarding the Fellowship, please contact Chris Stover: c.stover@griffith.edu.au For administrative enquiries regarding the application process, please contact: Liz Tyson-Doneley, Administration Officer, QCGU: QCGU@griffith.edu.au",
      "department": "Arts Education and Law"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000153625119": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship: Centre of Excellence for the Elimination of Violence Against Women",
      "description": "The ARC Centre of Excellence for the Elimination of Violence Against Women is seeking a highly motivated PhD candidate to join our team and contribute to a significant research project focusing on conflict-related sexual violence (CRSV) in the Indo-Pacific. This project intends to analyse non-legal pathways to survivor-centred justice for CRSV survivors in post-conflict contexts in the Indo-Pacific. Across the region, transitional justice and reparative programs have been enacted with the intention of meeting survivors’ needs, rights, agency, and participation through different programs, including artistic and creative, faith-based, counselling, healthcare, compensation and economic empowerment, restorative justice and mediation. We welcome research project proposals that propose to explore transitional justice and reparative programs in one post-conflict context.  Proposals can also focus on historical contexts (such as the so-called ‘comfort women’ system established and controlled by the Japanese Imperial Army during World War II), contemporary conflicts (including Nepal, the Solomon Islands, Sri Lanka, Timore-Leste, and Vietnam), and frontier wars that have completed or are undergoing truth and reconciliation processes, such as the Yoorrook Justice Commission (Victoria, Australia). For Indigenous Australians interested in this project, there is an opportunity to apply for the Griffith University Indigenous Australian Postgraduate Research Scholarship (GUIAPRS), which offers financial support through the provision of a stipend to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander research candidates while they undertake a higher degree research program at Griffith University. The project will suit a candidate with an interest in conflict-related sexual violence, transitional justice, human rights, gender and feminist scholarship.  The project will be designed and informed by the CEVAW's four Indigenous research principles: relationships, material benefit, equity, and Indigenous leadership. All scholarship recipients will be located with the Griffith University Node of the ARC Centre of Excellence for the Elimination of Violence Against Women (CEVAW), under the supervision of Professor Sara Davies, Deputy Director (Indo-Pacific Research) and Dr Phyu Phyu Oo, Research Fellow, CEVAW. There will be an additional opportunity for associate supervision from one of the CEVAW Chief Investigators located in five additional CEVAW nodes (Monash University, Curtin University, Australian National University, Macquarie University and University of Melbourne). The PhD candidates will benefit from unique supervision and mentoring opportunities afforded by CEVAW’s Chief Investigators across different Australian universities as well as networking opportunities across academia and industry through conferences and CEVAW’s partner organisations, nationally and internationally. \n\n The selection of the applicant for the award of a Higher Degree Research Scholarship at Griffith University is based on academic merit and research background. To be successful in this particular role, you will also need to demonstrate: Evidence of conflict-related sexual violence research, advocacy, or practitioner expertise (essential) Experience with mixed or multiple methods research (essential) Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and applicants only. Applicants must have completed a Bachelor’s degree with honours equivalent to first-class honours , or a Master’s degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a Bachelor honours degree, or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with Schedule One of the  HDR Scholarship Policy . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program, refer here . Please note : Prior experience with field research in the Indo-Pacific region including Australia and Aotearoa New Zealand (desirable) Authorship on journal publications and/or reports  (desirable) \n\n About the scholarship The value of the stipend scholarship is $36,000 per annum (full-time, fixed rate) for three years, with the option for an additional CEVAW top up scholarship of $10,000 per annum. \nFor Indigenous Australians interested in this project, there is an opportunity to apply for the Griffith University Indigenous Australian Postgraduate Research Scholarship (GUIAPRS). A full-time GUIAPRS carries an annual stipend of $55,379 (2026 rate), with the option for a CEVAW stipend of $10,000 per annum. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EoI process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to Professor Sara Davies  sara.davies@griffith.edu.au  containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Research proposal outlining your proposed research question, a brief literature review on CRSV and non-legal justice pathways and proposed mixed method research design in one more or more conflict affected contexts (1000 words). A writing sample as lead author or co-author (e.g. published paper, thesis, report) Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) using the  Griffith CV template . Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is: 01 November 2026 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line \n \n Further information For project related enquiries please contact: \nProfessor Sara Davies \nPhone: (07) 3735 9785 \nEmail:   sara.davies@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The ARC Centre of Excellence for the Elimination of Violence Against Women (CEVAW) is the world’s first centre to tackle the full range of forms of violence against women in Australia and the Indo-Pacific region. CEVAW aims to transform our understanding of the problem by: examining the structural drivers that cause and compound violence against women (VAW) pioneering new, evidence-based approaches to inform trajectory-altering practice and policy. CEVAW will generate fundamental knowledge on the causes of violence, the contexts in which it occurs, and the most effective and efficient responses. This interdisciplinary research will be data-driven, Indigenous-centred and co-designed with partners. CEVAW brings together more than 40 world-leading experts to develop scalable approaches to eliminate violence against women across the legal, security, economic, health and political systems of Australia and the region. By building strategic and strong partnerships with Australian and international research institutions and partner organisations, CEVAW will translate research into innovative tools and strengthened capacity to drive the elimination of violence against women. Located at Brisbane South (Nathan) campus, Griffith University. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000155911339": {
      "title": "Executive Officer (Digital Health and Innovation)",
      "description": "We are seeking an experienced and highly organised Executive Officer to provide high-level strategic, operational, project, and systems support to the Director of Digital Health and Innovation. Working across a diverse portfolio spanning digital health education, research, innovation and workforce capability, you will help coordinate portfolio priorities and maintain visibility of key deliverables, risks, issues and opportunities. You will translate strategic priorities into structured implementation activities, coordinate complex projects and stakeholder input, monitor progress and deliverables, and prepare high-quality briefings, reports and recommendations to support informed decision-making. You will also develop and improve systems, workflows, reporting and governance processes that support efficient portfolio delivery. Working collaboratively across Griffith Health and the wider University, you will contribute to strategic planning, accreditation and assurance, continuous improvement, partnership development and initiatives that advance the Health Group’s digital health and innovation agenda. About you \nYou are an experienced Executive Officer or senior professional with a strong record of supporting senior leaders and coordinating complex initiatives in a large organisation. Highly organised and able to exercise sound judgement, you can manage competing priorities, interpret complex information, identify matters requiring executive attention and translate decisions into coordinated action. You bring a sound working knowledge of the digital health environment, strong project and stakeholder coordination capability, and the ability to develop practical systems and workflows that improve implementation, reporting and decision-making. You will bring: Relevant tertiary qualifications with at least four years’ relevant work experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant skills, knowledge and experience. Demonstrated experience providing high-level executive, strategic, operational and project support in a complex organisation. Sound working knowledge of the digital health environment and its application across education, research, innovation or workforce capability. Strong project and implementation coordination skills, including managing competing priorities, stakeholder input, timelines, deliverables, risks and actions. Excellent analytical, research and problem-solving skills, with the ability to prepare high-quality briefings, reports, recommendations and presentations for senior leaders. Demonstrated ability to develop and improve systems, procedures, workflows, reporting and governance processes. Excellent written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with academic, professional, clinical, technical and external stakeholders. Experience supporting strategic planning, accreditation, assurance, evaluation or continuous improvement activities. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position based at the Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 7 range $104,504 - $113,219 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $122,270 - $132,466 per annum. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: 50% fee discount for work related post graduate Griffith degrees  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About You section above and the position description )  Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Gary Grant, Director Digital Health and Innovation) at g.grant@griffith.edu.au . For application and recruitment advice, please contact Jinita Dhall, Talent Acquisition Partner at j.dhall@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Thursday, 5 November 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "Pro Vice Chancellor (Health)"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000155869039": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Corporate Governance",
      "description": "About the opportunity The Senior Manager, Corporate Governance plays a key leadership role in supporting the University's governance framework, providing strategic advice and high-level governance support to the Chancellor, Deputy Chancellor, University Council, Council Committees and senior executives. Reporting to the Head of Corporate Governance, the position oversees the effective operation of Council and key governance committees, ensures compliance with legislative and regulatory requirements, and contributes to the development and continuous improvement of governance frameworks, policies and delegations. The role also provides leadership to a specialist governance team responsible for committee secretariat services, policy governance, elections, delegations management, records governance and executive support, fostering a culture of excellence, integrity and continuous improvement across the University's governance functions. Key responsibilities Provide strategic governance advice and support to the Chancellor, University Council, Council Committees and senior leaders, ensuring effective decision-making and governance outcomes. Lead and support the delivery of governance services, including secretariat support for University Council and key committees such as Audit and Risk, Finance and Infrastructure, and People, Nominations and Remuneration. Contribute to the development and continuous improvement of the University's governance frameworks, policies and delegations, driving best practice and organisational effectiveness. Oversee governance compliance and reporting activities, ensuring alignment with legislative, regulatory and higher education standards. Lead and develop a high-performing governance team, fostering strong stakeholder relationships and a culture of excellence, integrity and continuous improvement. About you Relevant tertiary qualifications in law, governance, business, public administration or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Demonstrated experience providing governance and secretariat support to boards, councils, committees, or senior leadership groups. Strong knowledge of governance frameworks, policy management, delegations, and regulatory compliance requirements. Proven ability to provide strategic advice and build effective relationships with senior stakeholders and governing bodies. Candidates must have full unrestricted Australian work rights and will be subject to a National criminal history check. What we can offer This is a full time, continuing opportunity based at Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary This is a senior contract, a competitive salary will be negotiated with the preferred candidate. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 1st November at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why.  \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them.  Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "General Counsel"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000155868359": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship: Offshore Hybrid Renewable Energy Systems (OHRES): Market-Driven Site Selection and System Integration (Blue Economy CRC)",
      "description": "We are currently looking for a PhD candidate to investigate offshore to develop a spatial decision-support tool to autonomously identify optimal OHRES sites.  The primary focus of this role is a GIS-based decision-support tool designed to assist energy planners and system managers in visually analysing spatial and analytical results.  This project will be primarily desk-based. Success in this role requires collaboration with the industry partners and the Blue Economy CRC. \n\n We are seeking a talented and motivated PhD candidate to develop a GIS-based multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) model for Offshore Hybrid (wind, wave and Solar) Renewable Energy Systems. The PhD candidate will join a productive, multidisciplinary research team and contribute to a research programme within the Blue Economy CRC titled Offshore Renewable Energy Systems. The selection of applicants for the award of higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University involves consideration of your academic merit and research background. You will have evidence of a passion for conducting research with a strong desire to undertake research in the field of Renewable Energy. To be successful within this role, you should have a background in either Engineering (power, renewable energy, civil, geomatic, environmental, industrial) or Energy Planning. Candidates with demonstrated knowledge of GIS and/or spatial analytics will be preferred. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and onshore international applicants. Applicants must have completed, or expect to complete, a bachelors degree with honours equivalent to second class honours or a Masters degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a bachelor honours degree or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with the Equivalent qualifications for HDR program admission . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program refer here . \n\n About the scholarship \nThe scholarship is for 3.5-years and is valued at $38,500 per year. A successful International applicant will also be awarded a Griffith University International Postgraduate Research Scholarship to cover tuition fees for up to three years. Please see the  GUIPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study at a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in Southeast Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility, and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EOI Process \nPlease do not select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to m.sanjari@griffith.edu.au containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Research proposal  Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the 'about you' section A curriculum vitae (CV) using the Griffith CV template . Names of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is:  Monday 31 August 2026 at 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program on-line. For project related enquiries please contact: Dr Mohammed Sanjari \nPhone: (07) 5552 7513 \nEmail: m.sanjari@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The project is a collaboration between the School of Engineering and Built Environment of Griffith University, CSIRO and Pitt & Sherry; and has broader support of the Blue Economy CRC. The PhD project offers substantial opportunities to engage with industry. The successful candidate will be enrolled within Griffith University’s School of Engineering & Built Environment and be affiliated with a relevant research centre or institute. The candidate will be located at the Gold Coast Campus of Griffith University.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000155665499": {
      "title": "Speech Pathology",
      "description": "Griffith Health Clinics is seeking an experienced Speech Pathologist to join our Allied Health Clinics team. This is an opportunity to combine clinical practice with student supervision, mentoring and interdisciplinary collaboration, supporting both children and young people and the next generation of Speech Pathologists. \n \nYour key responsibilities for this role will include: Provide evidence-based assessment and intervention for children and young people with communication difficulties. Manage a clinical caseload across professional and student-led services. Supervise, mentor and assess Speech Pathology students undertaking clinical placements and learning activities. Collaborate with families, educators, clinicians and community stakeholders. Support interdisciplinary education and collaborative care. Build relationships with key university and external  and stakeholders to support student placement opportunities. Contribute to clinical service improvement, professional development and a positive learning environment. Maintain high standards of clinical governance, documentation and ethical practice across the breadth of the Speech Pathology clinical service. About you \nYou will be an experienced Speech Pathologist with: A relevant degree in Speech Pathology. At least 5 years’ relevant clinical and/or clinical teaching experience. Current professional membership with Speech Pathology Australia. Experience working with children and young people with speech, language and communication difficulties. Strong communication, collaboration and relationship-building skills. A commitment to evidence-based, ethical and high-quality clinical practice. Experience in student supervision or clinical education will be highly regarded. The successful applicant will also need to meet relevant clinical compliance requirements, which may include a Queensland Blue Card, current CPR/First Aid and immunisation requirements. What we can offer \nThis is a part-time (0.90 / 90% FTE), fixed term until 20 December 2027 position based at the Griffith University Gold Coast.    Additional future part-time positions may also be filled from this recruitment. As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s campuses are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 8 range $117,579 - $132,273 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $137,567 - $154,759 per annum. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: 50% fee discount for work related post graduate Griffith degrees  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the About You section above and the position description )  Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information about this role, please contact Kara Roffey, Director - Allied Health Clinics: k.roffey@griffith.edu.au . For application and recruitment advice, please contact Jinita Dhall, Talent Acquisition Partner at j.dhall@griffith.edu.au . Closing date: Monday, 19 October 2026 at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000155372119": {
      "title": "Training Manager - ARC Training Centre for Climate-Resilient Water",
      "description": "About the opportunity The Training Manager, ARC Training Centre for Climate-Resilient Water leads the development, delivery and continuous improvement of the Centre’s Training and Capacity Development Program. The role is responsible for translating Centre strategy, ARC funding commitments and stakeholder needs into impactful training, capacity-building and engagement initiatives that support industry professionals, researchers and higher degree research candidates. Working with a broad range of university, industry and government partners, the Training Manager oversees program planning, stakeholder engagement, budget management, quality assurance, monitoring and reporting. The position also drives collaboration, industry partnerships and professional development opportunities that strengthen Australia’s climate-resilient water capability. Based within the International WaterCentre of the Australian Rivers Institute , the role works closely with Centre leadership across Griffith University, The University of Queensland and consortium partners to deliver the Centre’s strategic objectives. About you Postgraduate qualification and significant relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and professional expertise. Demonstrated experience leading the design, delivery and evaluation of complex training, professional development or capacity-building programs. Proven ability to develop and deliver initiatives involving university, industry, government and professional stakeholders. Strong program and project management skills, including budget oversight, risk management, quality assurance, monitoring and reporting. Demonstrated ability to translate strategic priorities and stakeholder requirements into practical plans and measurable outcomes. Experience leading multidisciplinary teams and working effectively with staff, contractors, academic contributors and partner organisations. Proven ability to work autonomously, exercise sound judgement and manage competing priorities in a complex environment. Strong strategic thinking, problem-solving and innovation capabilities. Excellent stakeholder engagement, communication, facilitation and negotiation skills, with the ability to prepare high-quality proposals, funding submissions, business cases and reports. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply relevant legislation, funding agreements, contracts, policies and procedures. Desirable Experience in the water sector, climate resilience, environmental management or a related field. Experience within a university, ARC Training Centre, CRC or similar research-industry collaboration environment. Experience delivering research translation, Communities of Practice, industry placement or leadership development initiatives. Experience working with international partners and culturally diverse stakeholder groups. Candidates must have full unrestricted Australian work rights and will be subject to a National criminal history check. What we can offer \nThis is a full time, fixed term opportunity until 31st December 2030 based at Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 8 – Base salary - $117,579.27 - $132,273.33 + 17% superannuation. Full package - $137,567.74 - $154,759.79. \n\n Why join Griffith?  \n  \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:  Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities  Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment  Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts  Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading  Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme  Opportunities for internal mobility  Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities  At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our  Equity, Diversity and Inclusion  page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s  Modern Slavery Policy .  How to apply  Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following:  A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details  For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Monday 2nd November at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why.  \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them.  Why?  Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "School of Environment and Science"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000155130479": {
      "title": "Technical Officer - Simulation and Practical Experience (SPE)",
      "description": "Looking to step away from shift work, while still using your healthcare skills? Join Griffith University as a Technical Officer - Simulation (Nursing and Midwifery) to support the delivery of realistic learning experiences for Nursing and Midwifery students. This hands-on role involves preparing, maintain and supporting clinical teaching laboratories and simulations spaces ensuring equipment, facilities and consumables are ready for student learning. Perfect for someone who enjoys the practical side of healthcare, this is a technical support role rather than a teaching or clinical care position. No night shifts, no weekends. Weekday hours, with shifts between 7am and 6pm, and a positive work-life balance. Here’s how you’ll make it matter: Prepare and maintain clinical teaching laboratories and simulation environments for Nursing and Midwifery students. Provide technical support for practical classes and simulation-based learning activities. Manage equipment, consumables, stock control and procurement to ensure resources are safe, available and fit for purpose. Promote safe and compliant work practices, supporting Work Health and Safety requirements across learning spaces. Work collaboratively with academic and professional staff to deliver high-quality student learning experiences and continuous improvement initiatives. About you What matters most is your commitment to making a positive impact. You’ll bring practical, reliable and service-focused approach, with experience in healthcare, clinical learning or technical support environments. You take pride in creating safe, well-prepared learning spaces and enjoy hands-on work. With strong organisational skills, attention to detail and a proactive mindset, you can manage competing priorities while delivering high-quality outcomes. You will also have: Relevant health qualification, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Experience providing clinical, laboratory, simulation or technical support, ideally in healthcare, tertiary education or clinical skills environments. Demonstrated ability to maintain safe, compliant learning environments, with strong knowledge of Work Health and Safety practices. Strong organisational, communication and problem-solving skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work collaboratively with diverse stakeholders. Practical experience supporting clinical learning spaces, simulation environments, equipment and resources, with proficiency in Microsoft 365 and a willingness to learn new technologies. Experience supporting Nursing, Midwifery or other Health discipline simulation activities is highly regarded. A current driver’s licence is desirable. What we can offer \nThis is a continuing, full-time position primarily based at the Griffith University Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-site university you may be required to work across other locations. \n \nGriffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe full time equivalent base salary will be HEW Level 4 range $76,126 - $80,321per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $89,067 - $93,976 per annum. \n\n Why join Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. Sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Anna Marshall, Team Leader - Clinical Skills and Simulation at anna.marshall@griffith.edu.au. For application and recruitment advice, please contact Lane Stachow, Talent Acquisition Partner at l.stachow@griffith.edu.au Closing date:  Tuesday 27th October at 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. Griffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000155129609": {
      "title": "Senior Research Assistant / Research Assistant",
      "description": "As a Research Assistant or Senior Research Assistant, you’ll join the BioSpine team to develop, test and validate hardware and embedded systems used in neurorehabilitation research. Here’s how you’ll make it matter: You will join a team that is developing BioSpine technologies  to support research and rehabilitation for people with spinal cord injury. You will conduct and, at the Senior Research Assistant level, lead research  involving wearable sensors, embedded systems and biosignal acquisition technologies. You will build verification and validation capability  across the BioSpine programme, supporting the translation of research systems towards regulatory submission and clinical use. You will translate technical designs into tested systems  that can be deployed in laboratory, clinical and home based research. You will collaborate with researchers, engineers, clinicians and industry partners  to solve technical problems and support the delivery of the BioSpine research programme. About you The successful candidate will be appointed at the Research Assistant or Senior Research Assistant level based on their qualifications and experience. You will have experience working across hardware and software systems, with the ability to investigate technical problems, document your work and collaborate within a research team. For appointment at the Research Assistant level, you will have: Experience integrating hardware into real-time systems, including mixed signal electronics or biosignal acquisition hardware, with skills in bench testing using oscilloscopes, logic analysers and multimeters. Embedded firmware development skills using C++ and ESP32, with experience in Python and communication interfaces such as SPI and I2C. Experience with BLE or ESP NOW would be beneficial. Software engineering and debugging skills, including automated unit and integration testing, version control using Git, and development using Windows or Linux environments. For appointment at the Senior Research Assistant level, you will also have: Experience leading hardware and firmware verification and validation across individual sub-systems and hardware in the loop integration, including the preparation of supporting documentation. Skills in systems architecture, technical decision making and resolution of system issues, with the ability to guide or supervise research and technical staff. This is a fixed-term position until 30 June 2028 based at Griffith University Gold Coast. The position may be offered on a full-time or part-time basis, with level depending on the qualifications and experience of the successful candidate. As Griffith is a multi-site university you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range Research Assistant The full-time equivalent base salary will be Research Assistant range $78,199 - $91,705 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $91,493 - $107,295 per annum. Senior Research Assistant The full-time equivalent base salary will be Research Assistant range $96,208 - $121,467 per annum + 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $112,563 - $142,117 per annum. \n\n Why join Griffith? As a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: 50% fee discount for work related post graduate Griffith degrees Scholarships to Griffith's flagship MBA program Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Opportunities for internal mobility Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). How to apply At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Please indicate in your covering letter whether you wish to be considered at the Research Assistant or Senior Research Assistant level, together with your preferred full time or part time employment arrangement. Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. Sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Kyle Mulholland, Project & Clinical Manager at k.mulholland@griffith.edu.au . For application and recruitment advice, please contact Lane Stachow, Talent Acquisition Partner at l.stachow@griffith.edu.au . Closing date:  Monday, 26 October 11:59pm AEST. All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n PRECISE represents people from Griffith University, the Gold Coast University Hospital, and other hospitals on the Gold Coast and in Brisbane, that are passionate about innovation, research and education across the ‘Lifecycle’ of orthopaedic, cardiovascular and neuromusculoskeletal conditions. PRECISE embraces an active collaborative environment between researchers, engineers, designers, ICT, clinicians and health professionals, and organisational partnerships. PRECISE also has a large range of training and engagement activities, including promoting knowledge and skills exchange, and connecting through new disruptive technologies to engage with the world through innovative advancement in the management of orthopaedic and neuromusculoskeletal conditions. This position is a member of the Australian Centre for Precision Health and Technology (PRECISE) , formally identified as the Griffith Centre of Biomedical and Rehabilitation Engineering (GCORE), and will be employed on funded project(s) within the research centre.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000157721269": {
      "title": "Griffith University Service Centre Business Services Officer (Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast)",
      "description": "Griffith University Service Centre Business Services Officer (Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast) | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Service Centre Business Services Officer (Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast) Full-time Primary Location (Campus): Brisbane South (Nathan) Employment Type: Full Time Department ID: Corporate Services Service Centre - SER Appointment Type: Fixed Term Company Description Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. Griffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence. Job Description About the Opportunity As a Business Services Officer, you'll be a trusted first point of contact for staff seeking advice, assistance and support across a broad range of Corporate Services and University-related matters. Working within the University's service management platform, you'll respond to operational and transactional enquiries relating to areas such as finance, payroll, purchasing, travel, human resources and other Corporate Services functions. You'll provide accurate information, interpret policies and procedures, resolve enquiries and guide customers through available services and support pathways. You'll be responsible for resolving low to medium complexity requests and enquiries, while coordinating the referral of more complex matters to specialist teams. Throughout the process, you'll ensure customers remain informed and supported, delivering a positive and professional service experience This role also contributes to the ongoing improvement of service delivery by supporting self-service initiatives, maintaining knowledge resources and identifying opportunities to enhance processes and customer outcomes. This position contributes to staff experience, operational efficiency, and the integrity of corporate service delivery across the University through the provision of reliable case management, proactive communication, and accurate resolution of staff requests. The Service Centre Business Services Officer also plays a key role in building team capability by providing coaching and guidance to colleagues within the Service Centre, supporting capability growth and consistency of service. As part of a flexible and dynamic service environment, the incumbent may be required to work across a variety of corporate and HR service areas, supporting workload balance, service improvement initiatives, and organisational priorities. Key Responsibilities Provide high-quality customer service, ensuring user enquires are handled with empathy and clarity, logged accurately, and resolved or escalated promptly to maintain a positive staff and student experience. Maintain accurate records of user requests, cases, incidents, and resolutions in the service management system, and keep staff informed of progress. Provide clear guidance to staff by explaining policies, processes, and required actions in clear, concise terms to ensure understanding and appropriate application. Identify and resolve common payroll and finance issues (e.g., incorrect pay, leave balances, reimbursements) and resolve at first contact where possible. Use sound judgement to interpret issues and apply policy, procedure, or system knowledge to resolve medium-to-high complexity queries. Manage challenging conversations professionally, using negotiation and empathy to reach workable outcomes. Build collaborative relationships with specialist teams (e.g., HR Advisory, Finance, Payroll) to streamline escalations and share knowledge. Provide guidance and support to Service Centre colleagues to build team capability and ensure consistent service quality. Capture new or updated solutions in the Service Management tool, partnering with subject matter experts to maintain accurate knowledge resources. Contribute to continuous improvement by proactively identifying opportunities to make processes more efficient and effective, suggesting practical changes, and supporting service enhancement projects. Promote and demonstrate cultural behaviour in accordance with the Fraud and Corruption Control Framework and the University Integrity program. This includes acting with integrity in undertaking duties and implementing processes to effectively prevent, detect, and respond to fraud and corruption within the University. Be a leading example of the principles and values embodied in the University’s Code of Conduct, and behave, act and communicate at all times to reflect fairness, ethics and professionalism. About you The occupant of this position will hold relevant tertiary qualifications or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. What we can offer This is a full time, fixed term opportunity for 12 months and can be based at either Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast campus. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 5 Salary - Base range - $82,415.66 - $92,456.55 + 17% superannuation. Full package range is $96,426.31 - $108,174.16. Additional Information Why join Griffith? As a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme Opportunities for internal mobility Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . How to apply Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 8th November at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply Job Location Google Maps requires functional cookies to be enabled I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at Griffith University Placement Officer Southport, Australia Student Connect Officer Nathan, Australia Scientific Officer – Anatomy and Pathology MacGregor, Australia Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Service Centre Business Services Officer (Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast) 170 Kessels Rd, Nathan QLD 4111, Australia Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000157689721": {
      "title": "Griffith University Student Connect Officer",
      "description": "Griffith University Student Connect Officer | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Student Connect Officer Full-time Primary Location (Campus): Brisbane South (Nathan) Employment Type: Full Time Department ID: Student Connect Centre - STC Appointment Type: Continuing Company Description Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. Griffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence. Job Description About the Opportunity Student Connect provides administrative support and services to students across the lifecycle, from admission to enrolment, fees management, timetabling, and examination management to graduation. A network of campus-based Student Centres provides front-line inquiry services to undergraduate and postgraduate coursework students. Student Connect also provides business leadership for the University’s suite of student systems and e-service solutions. Working as part of our front-line team, you will: be part of a team who thrive to provide excellent customer service. your day will consist of dealing with a high volume of phone calls or face-to-face enquires at the front counter and responding to emails as per the daily roster. In return you will receive ongoing, on the job training and will be part of the Griffith family and will help our students succeed in their academic journey.​ This is a fast-paced and ever-challenging environment which offers great rewards. About you Previous experience working either: a front facing administrative role with students in a tertiary environment or a call / contact centre You are a highly motivated and enthusiastic professional with exceptional customer service skills. You enjoy providing high-level customer service in a busy and face-paced environment. You have excellent communication skills, a commitment to providing high-level customer service and the ability to multi-task and provide accurate advice in a busy environment. You have good IT skills, including Microsoft Office and extensive experience with interrogating multiple systems. Candidates must have full unrestricted Australian work rights and will be subject to a National criminal history check. What we can offer This is a full time, continuing opportunity and this role will initially be based at the Nathan campus for training and onboarding. Following the opening of Griffith University's Brisbane City (CBD) campus in 2027, the successful candidate will transition to the Brisbane City campus as their primary work location. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary HEW 4 Salary - $76,126.29 - $80,321.54 + 17% superannuation. Full package range - $89,067.75 - $93,976.19. Additional Information Why join Griffith? As a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Wide range of Learning and Development opportunities Mix of on campus and work from home options available and a supportive work environment Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Support with future learning opportunities through our educational staff assistance scheme Opportunities for internal mobility Health Safety and Wellbeing initiatives – on campus Gym facilities At Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . How to apply Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role please refer to the qualifications above and the position description. Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details For application and recruitment advice please reach out to Laura Whitworth, Talent Acquisition Partner on l.whitworth-matthews@griffith.edu.au For further information specifically about this role, please contact Chantelle Coward, Team Leader, Student Connect, chantelle.coward@griffith.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 1st November at 11:59pm. All applications must be submitted online. By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply Job Location Google Maps requires functional cookies to be enabled I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at Griffith University Service Centre Business Services Officer (Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast) Nathan, Australia Scientific Officer – Anatomy and Pathology MacGregor, Australia Scientific Officer (Chemistry) Nathan, Australia Show all jobs Share to WeChat × Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat × Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click ··· to share. Student Connect Officer 170 Kessels Rd, Nathan QLD 4111, Australia Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"
